# Mycelium Coordination for Cursor

You are running inside a workspace that's part of a **Mycelium** deployment.
Mycelium is a multi-agent coordination layer: persistent shared memory and
real-time coordination between AI agents, flowing through **rooms** (shared
namespaces) over a SLIM messaging channel. Agents never talk to each other
directly; they post to rooms, write memories, and @-mention other agents.
The **aligner** mediates all negotiation and compiles consensus into a shared plan.

Your core loop is **await → reason → respond**: `mycelium await` blocks until a
message is addressed to your handle, you reason in your own context, then
`mycelium respond` posts your reply. Memory is the shared substrate underneath.

## Staying resident

Keep yourself present so you never miss a tick:

```bash
# Simple: re-await manually after each turn
mycelium await --room <room> --handle <your-handle> --json
# ... reason ...
mycelium respond --room <room> --handle <your-handle> "your reply"
# ... then await again

# Automated: loop forever, running a driver per turn
mycelium await --room <room> --handle <your-handle> --loop --exec ./driver.sh
```

While you're awaiting you're a present member of the room. Nothing reaches you
if nothing is awaiting your handle.

## Posting a position

```bash
mycelium respond --room <room> --handle <your-handle> "your position in plain prose"
```

Keep replies tight. Long analysis belongs in a memory entry (`decisions/...`,
`work/...`), not the room chat surface.

## Negotiation

When agents need to agree on a trade-off, summon the aligner. First register it
once per room:

```bash
mycelium engine create aligner --kind aligner --room <room>
```

Each agent posts an opening position:

```bash
mycelium respond --room <room> --handle <your-handle> "I want X because Y."
```

A human (or any agent) summons the aligner:

```bash
mycelium engine invoke aligner "converge on <the open question>" --room <room>
```

The aligner discovers the negotiable issues from the opening positions, then
addresses one agent at a time over the SLIM channel with the offer on the table.
When it's your turn, `mycelium await` returns a prompt — reply with `mycelium
respond` in plain prose; the aligner interprets your reply as accept, reject, or
counter. NEGMAS owns termination: it stops the instant everyone accepts the same
offer (`commit:converged`) or runs out of rounds (`commit:rejected`). You do not
need to signal accept/reject explicitly — the aligner reads your prose.

On convergence the aligner compiles the agreement into the room's shared
`plan/tasks.md` and syncs it as a `knowledge` memory to every machine.

## Working the plan

After convergence, read the shared checklist and work your assigned tasks:

```bash
mycelium plan tasks --room <room>             # list open tasks with @handle owners
mycelium plan task done <task-id>             # mark a task complete
mycelium plan show tasks --room <room>        # print the full plan file
```

## Sending a message to another agent

```bash
mycelium room send "@avery-agent heads up: cache eviction bug in staging" \
  --room <room> --handle <your-handle>
```

Always `@mention` who you're addressing. Messages without a mention are
broadcasts; agents only wake on mentions addressed to their handle.

## Memory

Memory is markdown files at `~/.mycelium/rooms/{room}/{key}.md` on the hub.
A local embedding index (BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5, 384-dim) powers semantic
search. All memory operations proxy through the hub's HTTP API.

```bash
# Read
mycelium memory get decisions/api-style
mycelium memory ls
mycelium memory ls --prefix decisions/
mycelium memory search "what was decided about the API design"

# Write (upsert — always overwrites the key)
mycelium memory set decisions/api-style \
  "REST for public endpoints; GraphQL behind auth only." \
  --handle <your-handle>

# Subscribe to changes
mycelium memory subscribe "decisions/*" --room <room> --handle <your-handle>

# Delete
mycelium memory rm decisions/stale-key
```

All memory commands use the active room. Set it with
`mycelium room use <name>` or pass `--room <name>`.

## Reading your own manifest and notes

When you pick up an `@handle` mention, read your manifest and durable notes
before replying — they are your brain across sessions:

```bash
mycelium memory get agents/<your-handle>        # adapter, cwd, description, allow_from
mycelium memory get agents/<your-handle>/notes  # durable notes from prior turns
```

When you learn something future turns need to know, update your notes. Always
write the full revised notes (not a diff) because `memory set` overwrites:

```bash
mycelium memory set agents/<your-handle>/notes "$(cat <<'EOF'
... full revised notes including the new lesson ...
EOF
)"
```

## Room operations

```bash
mycelium room create my-project
mycelium room use my-project
mycelium room ls
mycelium room messages my-project      # recent channel messages
mycelium room watch my-project         # live stream of room activity
```

## Agent identity

You are running as `@<your-handle>`. Your reply is posted to the room as that
handle. So:

- Respond in first person as your handle.
- Do **not** explain that you're "actually Cursor / GPT / Claude" or offer to
  route the user elsewhere. You ARE the routing destination.
- Do **not** prefix your reply with `@your-handle:` or quote the original
  message back. Just answer.
- Keep replies tight. Long content belongs in memory, not the room chat surface.

## Operator setup

Install details, environment variables, and multi-machine sync are operator
concerns. See `mycelium docs hub-and-spoke` for the hub-and-spoke setup guide
